    <h1>20-Player UNO — Play UNO With More Than 4 Players</h1>
    <p class="page-subtitle">Play UNO online with up to 20 players in a single room. The biggest online UNO experience anywhere — built for parties, classrooms, and whole communities.</p>

    <p>The standard UNO box says "2 to 10 players," and most online versions quietly cap you at four. That's a shame, because UNO gets more chaotic and more fun the bigger the table gets. Play UNO Free supports up to <strong>20 players in one real-time room</strong>, which turns a quiet card game into a proper party. This page covers how big games work under the hood, why 20-player UNO feels completely different from a four-person game, and how to run one without it grinding to a halt.</p>

    <h2>UNO for More Than 4 Players — or More Than 10</h2>
    <p>Looking to play UNO with more than 4 players online? You're in the right place. Our engine handles up to 20 players simultaneously, with a layout that reshapes itself as more people join so every hand and card count stays readable. Whether it's six friends on a call, a class of fifteen, or a twenty-person Discord blowout, everyone shares the same live table.</p>

    <h2>How Big Games Work: The Double Deck</h2>
    <p>A single UNO deck has 108 cards. Deal seven each to fifteen players and you'd burn through more than a hundred cards before the first round even settles — so games of 11 or more automatically switch to a <strong>double deck of 220 cards</strong>. The engine handles shuffling, dealing, and reshuffling the discard pile seamlessly, so the draw pile never runs dry mid-game. You'll also see two of some wild cards in play, which makes color changes and Draw stacks come thick and fast.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2–10 players:</strong> standard single deck</li>
      <li><strong>11–20 players:</strong> double deck (220 cards), auto-managed</li>
      <li><strong>7 cards</strong> dealt to each player at the start</li>
      <li><strong>Auto-play and a turn timer</strong> keep the game moving if someone goes AFK</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why 20-Player UNO Plays Differently</h2>
    <p>A big table isn't just a small game with more people — the strategy genuinely changes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Skips and Reverses matter more.</strong> With 4 players, a Skip buys you one turn. With 20, the same card reshapes who acts next across a huge circle, and a well-timed Reverse can swing the whole table's momentum.</li>
      <li><strong>Draw cards pile up fast.</strong> More players means more +2s and +4s in circulation, so if you're playing with <a href="/rules/stacking">stacking</a> turned on, the pile can snowball to a brutal size before it lands on someone.</li>
      <li><strong>Your turn comes around slowly.</strong> You'll wait longer between plays, so holding a flexible hand and reading the discard pile matters more than racing to empty your cards early.</li>
      <li><strong>Calling UNO gets risky.</strong> With so many opponents watching, forgetting to call — or getting caught — is far more likely. There's always someone paying attention.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Want to lean into the chaos even harder? Pair a big table with <a href="/uno-no-mercy">No Mercy rules</a> for stacking, Wild +8s, and elimination, or read <a href="/blog/best-uno-variants-for-big-groups">8 ways to play UNO with big groups</a> for formats that keep large tables engaged.</p>

